  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0165 database contains 11 tables, each representing a different entity or relationship in the Olympic Games. The athlete table contains information about the athletes who participate in the games, including their name, gender, function, country, nationality, height, weight, disciplines, events, birth date, birth place, and more. The coach table contains information about the coaches who lead the athletes, including their name, gender, function, country, disciplines, and events. The event table contains information about the events that take place during the games, including the name, tag, sport, and more. The medalist table contains information about the athletes who win medals, including their name, gender, country, nationality, discipline, event, and more. The medal table contains information about the medals that are awarded, including the type, code, date, and more. The medal_total table contains information about the total number of medals awarded by country, including gold, silver, and bronze medals. The schedule table contains information about the schedule of the games, including the start and end dates, day, status, discipline, event, and more. The schedule_preliminary table contains information about the preliminary schedule of the games, including the start and end dates, estimated start time, venue, and more. The team table contains information about the teams that participate in the games, including their name, gender, country, disciplines, events, athletes, and more. The torch_route table contains information about the torch relay route, including the title, city, start and end dates, tag, and more. The venue table contains information about the venues where the events take place, including the name, sports, date, and more.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\nThe project focuses on developing a comprehensive data analytics and data warehouse solution for the Paris 2024 Olympic Summer Games. The system will manage and analyze data related to the event, encompassing participating countries, athletes, sports disciplines, medal standings, event details, and more. This repository provides a structured framework for processing and understanding extensive datasets associated with the 2024 Olympics.\n\n 2) Objectives\n- To create an efficient data warehouse for storing and managing data related to the Paris 2024 Olympics.\n- To provide tools and functionalities for analyzing various aspects of the Olympic data, such as athlete performance, medal standings, and event schedules.\n- To support data-driven decision-making and insights generation for stakeholders involved in the Olympics.\n\n 3) Stakeholders & Users\n- Olympic Organizing Committee: Oversees the setup and operation of the Olympics, using data for planning and logistics.\n- Athletes and Coaches: Access performance data and schedules for strategic preparation and participation.\n- Sports Analysts and Journalists: Utilize data insights for reporting and analysis of the event.\n- Fans and Spectators: Access event schedules and results.\n\n 4) Functional Descriptions\n- Athlete Management: Manage data related to athletes, including personal information, disciplines, and event participation.\n- Coach Management: Store and manage coach details and their roles within different sports disciplines.\n- Event Schedule and Results: Manage event scheduling, phases, and results tracking, including medal distribution.\n- Medal Tracking: Record and analyze medal standings by country and event.\n- Team Management: Manage data related to teams, including athletes and coaches details.\n- Venue and Torch Route Management: Track venues and torch relay routes with related details and scheduling.\n  \n 5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n- Scalability: The system should efficiently handle large volumes of data related to multiple aspects of the Olympic Games.\n- Performance: Ensure quick data retrieval and analysis capabilities.\n- Security: Implement robust security measures to protect sensitive data, including personal information of athletes and event details.\n- Usability: Provide user-friendly interfaces for different stakeholders to interact with the data system seamlessly.\n- Reliability: Ensure data accuracy and system uptime during the Olympic Games period for real-time access.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0165.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0165\nCREATE TABLE `athlete` (\n  `athlete_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `gender` char(1) NOT NULL,\n  `function` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `country` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `nationality` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `height` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `weight` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `disciplines` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `events` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `birth_date` date DEFAULT NULL,\n  `birth_place` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`athlete_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `coach` (\n  `coach_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `gender` char(1) NOT NULL,\n  `function` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `country` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `disciplines` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `events` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`coach_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `event` (\n  `event_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `tag` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `sport` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`event_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `medalist` (\n  `medalist_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `athlete_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `gender` char(1) NOT NULL,\n  `country` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `nationality` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `discipline` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `event` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`medalist_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `medalist_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`athlete_id`) REFERENCES `athlete` (`athlete_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `medal` (\n  `medal_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `code` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `date` date DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`medal_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `medal_total` (\n  `medal_total_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `country` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `gold` int(11) DEFAULT 0,\n  `silver` int(11) DEFAULT 0,\n  `bronze` int(11) DEFAULT 0,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`medal_total_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `schedule` (\n  `schedule_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `start_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `end_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `day` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `discipline` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `event` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`schedule_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `schedule_preliminary` (\n  `preliminary_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `start_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `end_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `estimated_start_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `venue` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`preliminary_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `team` (\n  `team_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `gender` char(1) NOT NULL,\n  `country` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `disciplines` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `events` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `athletes` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`team_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `torch_route` (\n  `route_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `city` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `start_date` date DEFAULT NULL,\n  `end_date` date DEFAULT NULL,\n  `tag` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`route_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `venue` (\n  `venue_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `sports` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `date` date DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`venue_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
